<p>President Donald Trump said he is considering “limited” military strikes on Iran as Washington tries to push Tehran toward an agreement that would curb Iran’s nuclear programme — even as the US continues a major military buildup in and around the Middle East. His comments come as indirect negotiations continue, with Iran’s foreign minister saying Tehran is preparing a draft counterproposal, or draft deal text, that could be ready within days to hand to US envoy Steve Witkoff. Reporting across several outlets says the US posture has widened to include major naval and air assets, including a carrier moving into the Mediterranean and an overall deployment described as among the region’s largest in decades. Analysts and diplomats quoted in Reuters-based reports warn the buildup is starting to crowd out diplomacy and raises the risk of miscalculation, even as both sides still point to a negotiated outcome as the goal. One encouraging sign in an otherwise tense standoff: several reports say the talks have moved far enough that both sides expect written proposals soon, keeping a diplomatic off-ramp in view despite the rhetoric and deployments.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/bLxouDxN_ZoVkUJavQVorKUrCts5v2GlCFIpQKacQzPTzVlKJA_LKs--p8yP9XYSk550JGiHwt7Y35w1v4C5SvrNk4CGcsIHubNLjTIrISWB' alt='US President Donald Trump speaks during a working breakfast with governors at the White House in Washington, DC on February 20, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Draft timeline: Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said Iran’s draft text could be ready in the next “two, three days” for internal review and, once approved by his superiors, passed to US envoy Steve Witkoff. He also suggested more meetings may be needed after the drafts are exchanged.</li><li>Deadline pressure: President Trump publicly suggested Iran had about 10 days to agree to a deal before facing possible action, according to reporting on his remarks.</li><li>Red-line dispute: Araghchi said US negotiators had not asked Iran for “zero enrichment,” which he said clashes with some US statements insisting Iran must not enrich uranium at any level.</li><li>Additional kit: Reporting citing US officials and public tracking said the Pentagon is sending more weaponry to the region beyond headline naval moves, including extra air defenses and submarines, as it prepares contingencies for possible strikes.</li><li>Basing constraints: Flight-tracking-based reporting said Saudi Arabia and the UAE will not allow US aircraft to use their airspace to strike Iran, pushing many fighters to operate from Jordan and increasing reliance on aerial refueling.</li></ul><blockquote>I guess I can say I am considering that. - Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian foreign ministry: Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi said a counterproposal, or draft text, could be ready within days. He framed the talks around keeping Iran’s enrichment peaceful rather than ending it outright. (<a href='https://www.ft.com/content/14c01257-f4dd-4f5a-ae10-e7134462135d'>Financial Times</a>)</li><li>White House: Press secretary Karoline Leavitt said talks had made “some progress” but that “a lot of details” still need to be worked through, as the administration continues moving military assets into the region. (<a href='https://www.allsides.com/news/2026-02-20-0800/middle-east-us-military-pushes-more-weaponry-middle-east-possible-strikes-iran'>AllSides</a>)</li><li>US officials (planning): Two US officials told Reuters that planning has reached an advanced stage, with options that could include targeting individuals and even pursuing leadership change if ordered. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/21/iran-nuclear-counterproposal-us-strikes-threat'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Trump advisers (politics): Some aides have urged President Trump to keep the focus on domestic economic concerns, with Reuters analysis highlighting the political risk of escalation ahead of US midterm elections. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/analysis-trump-pushes-us-toward-war-iran-advisers-urge-focus-economy'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Regional diplomats and analysts: Diplomats quoted in Reuters-based reporting said Gulf neighbours and Israel increasingly see conflict as more likely than a negotiated settlement as the US buildup accelerates, making diplomacy harder to sustain. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/analysis-us-and-iran-slide-towards-conflict-military-buildup-eclipses-talks'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/trump-can-attack-iran-at-any-moment-says-senior-trump-advisor-report-13982159.html'>Trump can attack Iran &#x27;at any moment&#x27;, says senior Trump advisor: Report</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/21/iran-nuclear-counterproposal-us-strikes-threat'>Iran preparing nuclear counterproposal as Trump warns he is considering limited military strikes</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/02/analysis-trump-pushes-us-toward-war-iran-advisers-urge-focus-economy'>Analysis-Trump pushes US toward war with Iran as advisers urge focus on economy</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-02-20/trump-confirms-us-is-considering-a-targeted-attack-against-iran.html'>Trump confirms US is considering a targeted attack against Iran</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/trump-pushes-us-toward-war-with-iran-as-advisers-urge-focus-on-economy'>Trump pushes US toward war with Iran as advisers urge focus on economy</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/2/20/trump-considering-limited-strikes-on-iran'>Trump ‘considering’ limited strikes on Iran</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/trump-suggests-limited-strikes-to-push-iran-to-nu